    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-162197",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4e551b65-4116-58bc-b2bc-e536930c69b4",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying the Types of Images Formed by an Affine Transformation",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097781/assets/c36f3eb07b45eebf7f4de2a26cb366ca4ab785b5c7e8e6d25eb7bbad2c418838.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nx=3u-v-1,\\,y=2u+5v.\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the rectangle shown above under the action of $T?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nThe given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v-1 \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ 2u+5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5-2\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =17 \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a non-singular matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is invertible, and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a parallelogram.",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A point",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A parallelogram",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A pentagon",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A line segment",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A triangle",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source_answer_policy": {
        "version": "revealed-ma-answer-v1",
        "feedback_sha256": "65079a77702baaed0fdbc2d51ff0713899210e4dc6c9448368b9cf31ed931cdb"
      },
      "difficulty": "easy",
