  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098036",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81507",
      "knowledge_point_id": "be9ac99c-424e-5e90-b26f-c26e4da704d7",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Final Position of a Particle",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis and its velocity at time $t>0$ is given by $v(t)={e}^{2-t}.$ At time $t=1$ the particle is located at the position $x=e.$ What is the position of the particle at time $t=2?$",
      "worked_solution": "The position of the particle at $t=2$ is equal to its position at $t=1$ plus the total displacement over $t\\in [1,2].$ Therefore, we can calculate it as follows:\n\n$$\nx(2)=x(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{2}v(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nCarrying out the computations, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2) & =e+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{e}^{2-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =e+{[-{e}^{2-t}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =e+([-{e}^{2-2}]-[-{e}^{2-1}]) \\\\  & =e+([-{e}^{0}]-[-{e}^{1}]) \\\\  & =e+(-1+e) \\\\  & =2e-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the position of the particle at $t=2$ is $x=2e-1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2e+2",
