  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098244",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-71793",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f3149834-ef92-5016-a84a-40b207fee519",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Open and Closed Sets of Real Numbers",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are closed?\n\nI. $[-2,2]$\n\nII. $[3,9]$\n\nIII. $[-1,1)\\cup (3,4]$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nAmong the given sets, only $[-2,2]$ and $[3,9]$ are closed because:\n\n- The boundary points of $[-2,2]$ are $-2$, and $2.$ Both of them lie in the set.\n\n- The boundary points of $[3,9]$ are $3$, and $9.$ Both of them lie in the set.\n\nThe other set is not closed because $[-1,1)\\cup (3,4]$ has a boundary point $1$, which does not lie in the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I, II and III",
